post_install() {
    cat <<'EOF'

quickshell-greetd installed.

The package does not create users, enable services or overwrite
/etc/greetd/config.toml. Do it deliberately:

  1. greetd ships the `greeter` user; confirm it can access seats:
       usermod -aG video,input,seat greeter

  2. Point greetd at the launcher (an example is installed next to it):
       cp /etc/greetd/config.toml.example /etc/greetd/config.toml

  3. Optional - turn an existing Quickshell shell into the login theme.
     The tree is copied, never modified. Add --appearance to also export the
     palette and wallpaper (credential-shaped config fields are redacted,
     because the theme directory is world-readable):
       qsgreet-theme-prepare ~/.config/quickshell/<shell> \
           --surface modules/iris/lock/IrisLockSurface.qml \
           --appearance \
           /usr/share/quickshell-greetd/themes/<name>
     then set QSGREET_THEME=<name> in /etc/quickshell-greetd/config.env.

     A prepared theme must be readable by the greeter user:
       chmod -R a+rX /usr/share/quickshell-greetd/themes/<name>

  4. Optional - set an avatar. ~/.face is invisible to the greeter because
     a home directory is mode 700, so it goes in a shared store:
       sudo qsgreet-set-avatar ~/Pictures/me.png

  5. Optional - fingerprint and keyring unlock. Both belong in greetd's PAM
     stack, where it already runs as root for the target user; the greeter
     gains no privilege. See:
       /usr/share/doc/quickshell-greetd/pam.d-greetd.example
     Keep a root TTY open while editing /etc/pam.d/greetd.

  6. Test before enabling, from a TTY:
       GREETD_SOCK= quickshell-greetd-launcher

With no theme configured the greeter shows its built-in login, and it falls
back to that login whenever a theme fails to load or fails to draw.

EOF
}

post_upgrade() {
    cat <<'EOF'

quickshell-greetd upgraded. Themes prepared by an older version must be
regenerated, because the injected entry and adapter files are versioned
alongside the core:

  qsgreet-theme-prepare <shell tree> --force /usr/share/quickshell-greetd/themes/<name>

EOF
}

pre_remove() {
    cat <<'EOF'

Remember to point /etc/greetd/config.toml at another greeter before
rebooting, otherwise greetd will have nothing to launch.

EOF
}
